  "overview": {
    "claims": [
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-542e0a5386dc-0000-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 1,
              "start": 1
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The case Inyo County v. Paiute\u2011Shoshone Indians was argued before the U.S. Supreme Court on March 31, 2003."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioners",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-542e0a5386dc-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Petitioners, the petitioners seek a rule that would make the tribe, its casinos, businesses, and personal property immune from search warrants and subpoenas in criminal investigations."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Reid Peyton Chambers",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-542e0a5386dc-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 31,
              "start": 31
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Reid Peyton Chambers, the tribe argues that tribal sovereign immunity, the principle that states may not infringe tribal self\u2011government, and the lack of a congressional authorization bar the warrant."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "United States (Amicus Curiae)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-542e0a5386dc-0001-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 21,
              "start": 21
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to United States (Amicus Curiae), the United States amicus brief states there is no federal regulation preventing the county or the State from sharing information, such as welfare applications, with the tribe."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Four States",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-542e0a5386dc-0002-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 43,
              "start": 43
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Four States, four states filed an amicus brief supporting the tribe\u2019s position."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-542e0a5386dc-0002-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 38,
              "start": 38
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The Court noted that Public Law 280 gives California criminal jurisdiction over offenses involving Indians but does not apply to tribes themselves."
      }
    ],
